A||||7||Lore||3.1 or 3.4||||1||
Z||000000||Lore||04-21-2001||03:03 AM||||I was wondering if anyone had some advice about this. Should I get a 2.8 bored out to a 3.1 or should I just get a 3.4. Which is the better power gain for the money and time spent? Also, does anyone know where I can find the focoa headers everyone talks about? Thanks<P><BR>Lore<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-21-2001||09:27 AM||||Everything you are wondering about boils down to one thing $$$$$.  If you do all the work yourself, it will take less $$.  <P>The 3.4 is the largest stock engine that can easily replace the 2.8.  The cost of the 3.4 depends on where you get it.  A new crate engine..$2000, a used crate engine.. $250 for a junk engine to $1000 for a good junk engine.  The same prices for a 3.1.  Any rebuild kit of a stock 2.8 to 3.1 will take time ($) and skill.<P>FOCOA headders are from Fiero Owners Club of America <A HREF="http://www.fieroowners.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroowners.com</A>  is the web address. Cost $550-590 and add on ceramic coating for another $190 or so.  (more $$)<P>No matter what you decide, plan on spending more $$ that you think.  As you get into the change, many unexpected problems will pop up.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou_dias||04-23-2001||10:18 AM||||You can buy the 3.1 components yourself for less than buying a "kit".  The only differnece in parts is the crank, pistons and rings.  However, an '88 or newer block has better oiling characteristics but there is a procedure for improving the older blocks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||04-23-2001||02:33 PM||||Someone asked almost the same question in the last 20 - 30  or so days.  I have done the 3.1 bore thing.  I and a few other people have put in their 2 cents to answer this.  I don't really want to retype everything but if you do a search, it should pop up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||04-23-2001||02:55 PM||||I do not think it just boils down to money...<BR>I was ready to take the plunge and go with a 3.4, then I thought about passing smog...<BR>In my area from my understanding regardless of what engine is in the car, my car must emit bad things in accordance with a 2.8 litre v6.  Because I was unsure as to the output of a 3.4 I guessed that a 3.1 would likey have similiar emissions. Using data from my last smog test I should still be able to pass assuming a linear relation ship between displacement and emission values. <BR>A 3.4 would have bumped me over the magical smog values. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||04-23-2001||03:30 PM||||Greg,  I certainly hope that is not the way it is measured.  All the smog tests that I have ever seen show the emmission of the bad stuff as a percentage or ppm (parts per million).  That would imply that the little sniffer thing doesn't care aboout volume, but about statistical sampling of the stuff coming out of the pipe.<P>One thing I have seen is that a new cat can probably clean up any amount of pollutants coming out of a pipe, at least for a short time (long enough to pass the test anyway).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||04-23-2001||03:42 PM||||It was just a guess and its validity is not proven, but I would be really pissed if I got told that my car couldnt be driven b/c of the increase in displacement and emissions...  just me being paranoid likely...<P>But I know in california they are bastages about such things...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dave Gunsul||04-23-2001||10:52 PM||||Bob at Twin Lakes Fiero has done several 3.4<BR>conversions for some of the people in my club<BR>and they have all passed the eco-nazis tests.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
